                                       "kibana.version":  "9.4.2",
                                       "kibana.alert.rule.parameters":  {
                                                                            "description":  "Detects download or in-memory execution primitives in a PowerShell command, whether\nthey are hidden inside an encoded payload or written in plaintext on the command line.\n\nThis rule exists because measurement showed the plain encoded-command rule\n(1131fb39-a497-4a09-b051-4e4c89066f5f) cannot separate benign automation from an\nattack. In the lab\u0027s own alert population, 2 of 4 alerts were benign management\ntooling, and the remaining false positive shared its parent process, image, and\ncommand-line shape with the true positives. Nothing on the command line distinguishes\nthem, because the payload is base64. The discriminator is the payload itself, so it is\ndecoded at ingest by infra/elastic/ingest-pipelines/aegis-powershell-decode.json and\nmatched in DecodedCommand (that decoded text, not a Windows event field).\n\nA later Atomic run (T1027 #3) showed the decoded field is not enough on its own: an\nattacker who avoids -EncodedCommand and instead deobfuscates inline, for example\nIEX ([Convert]::FromBase64String(...)) read from the registry, leaves the primitives in\nplaintext where the decode pipeline never runs. The same vocabulary is therefore matched\non the raw command line as well. Measured against all lab telemetry the plaintext arm\nadded zero false positives.\n\nThe two rules are kept together on purpose and carry different severities: the plain\nencoded-command rule is context for hunting, and this one is the alerting rule.\n",
                                                                            "risk_score":  73,
                                                                            "severity":  "high",
